            <ArticleTitle>Stenosis of the external cervical os: an association with endometriosis in women with chronic pelvic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>571-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report a simple technique for diagnosing stenosis of the external cervical os and to evaluate an association between stenosis of the external cervical os and endometriosis in women with chronic pelvic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case series.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Academic medical center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-five women with chronic pelvic pain and stenosis of the external cervical os.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Measurement of the diameter of the external cervical os.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Association between cervical stenosis and surgically documented endometriosis in women with chronic pelvic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Endometriosis was visually documented at surgery for 24 of 25 women with chronic pelvic pain and stenosis of the external cervical os.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Endometriosis is a common finding in women with both stenosis of the external cervical os and chronic pelvic pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
